INT. SUDS & BUBBLES LAUNDROMAT - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent hum. Twelve dryers, one running. A bulletin board of faded flyers. Rain on the window. A broken vending machine glows.

MARISOL VEGA, 40s, scrubs, tired eyes, sits on a plastic chair with a phone in one hand, a crumpled envelope in the other. A laundry basket at her feet. She stares at the tumbling dryer as though it owes her money.

She sets the phone on her knee. Taps the screen. A voice memo plays.

MARISOL (V.O.)
(recording, shaky)
Hi, Mami. It's me. I know it's late.

Marisol closes her eyes.

MARISOL (V.O.)
(recording)
I got the results today. I wanted to tell you in person but I couldn't. I'm sorry.

She stops the playback. Opens her eyes. Looks at the envelope in her hand. Turns it over. Turns it back. She does not open it.

MARISOL
(to herself)
Open it.

She doesn't.

The dryer buzzes. She stands. Opens the door. Pulls out a bundle of warm towels. Buries her face in them. Breathes.

Behind her, the vending machine flickers. She folds a towel. Another. Her hands move fast, as if folding were a prayer she knows by heart.

MARISOL (CONT'D)
(to the empty room)
Okay.

She puts the envelope on top of the stack. Presses the stack flat with both hands. Stands there, shoulders high, not breathing, until the dryer clicks off.

She picks up the phone. Taps the screen. Holds it to her ear.

MARISOL (V.O.)
(recording, shaky)
I love you, Mami.

Marisol presses a button. The recording starts over.
